  "id": "halcyontowers",
  "neon": "#39f0ff",
  "note": "The west side’s signature tower. Twenty-one floors of\nsmoked-teal curtain glass on a helix — twenty plates, each turned about four degrees on the one\nbelow, so the corners walk three-quarters of the way round the building and every set-back catches\nthe light at a different angle. A brushed-metal collar at two mechanical floors breaks the glass,\nand the crown is a chamfered octagon (two boxes forty-five degrees apart) under a lit lantern and a\nneedle. Two light-runners trace opposite corners the whole way up, which is what the tower reads as\nafter dark.\n\n⚠ IT WAS HALF THIS HEIGHT FOR A WHILE, AND THAT WAS THE PORT RATHER THAN A DECISION. The luxtower\narm drew the mass to 2.9x the storey stack and the mast to 3.26x; the authored model that replaced\nit topped out at 1.18x and 1.42x, which is roughly the height every other authored model happens to\nuse. At this tile that is 0.758 against 1.862 in world-z before the bldgH/bldgStretch scale —\nSHORTER THAN THE SOLENNE (0.791), which has eight floors to this one’s twenty-one. It read as a\nstack of shoeboxes because it was one. The segments below put the mass back at 2.92x, tip 3.28x.\nThe shaft is a little wider than the arm’s was (0.36 against 0.32 at the foot, and a 0.44 taper\nagainst 0.48): at twenty-one floors the arm’s own proportion is a 19:1 pencil, and the brief for\nthis tower is grand as well as tall.\n\n⚠ AND THAT MAKES IT THE TALLEST THING IN COLDWATER, WHICH IS NOT WHERE IT BELONGS. The Spire has\nthirty floors to this one’s twenty-one and its own arm drew it to 18.1; the same porting pass cut\nthat one to 8.4 and nobody has put it back, so the two landmarks now stand in the wrong order\n(13.1 against 8.4). Restoring thespire.json the same way is what fixes it — shrinking this tower\nback would only reinstate the bug at a different building.\n\n⚠ AND THE LIGHT WENT WITH THE HEIGHT. The arm drew two spiralling corner runners and the authored\nformat had no way to say so, because every geometric scalar in it is affine in (fh, h) and a helix\nis not — so the ported tower was a dark column at night with a beacon on it. helixRunner is that\none missing kind; see its entry in building-model-schema.js.\n\n⚠ A YAWED BOX STILL LIGHTS ITSELF, which is the whole reason a tower can be built this way: a\nrotated segment still goes through draw3DBoxAt and keeps its curtain-wall texture and its lit\nfloors, where a drum in the same place would be a black column after dark. What it does NOT get is\ntrim — derivedTrim and anchored.mjs both take their wall planes from unyawed boxes only — so the\npodium is the one square unrotated mass in the model and every part of the detail list hangs on it.\n\n⚠ AND NONE OF THE TWISTED SEGMENTS IS OFFSET — see the warning in aurelia.json: a segment that is\nboth rotated AND shifted puts the derived kit where its own solid is not, and glself catches it.\n\n⚠ ty_chrome IS NOT A METAL HERE. It is in no *_WALL set, so it falls through to the default\nwindow-grid texture and a crown drawn in it comes out speckled brown. The collars and the crown use\nty_cold, which is in METAL_WALL. RENDER_TUNE.legacyArms puts the luxtower arm back.",
  "pal": "ty_halcyon",
